/-!
# Reflection identifies the antiholomorphic cusp-form summand

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.period_reflected_cusp_form`
(`a8792033-47ea-4c08-bbef-59a4cbb2477a`), by davidloeffler, 2026-09-07.

Proof: submission `a6c39f70-f2ea-4195-9d3b-e41dc4b072e7`, by davidloeffler, 2026-09-07 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

Let $N>0$, $k\ge2$, and $h\in S_k(\Gamma_1(N))$. Reflection defines a cusp form $v$ of the same
level and weight, with
$$v(z)=\overline{h(-\bar z)},\qquad v=0\Longleftrightarrow h=0.$$
This identifies the reflected period summand with an antiholomorphic differential. Specifically, if
$\rho=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1)$, $\jmath(z)=-\bar z$ and $n=k-2$, then pulling back
$h(z)(zX+Y)^n\,dz$ by $\jmath$ and applying $\rho$ to coefficients gives $-\overline{v(z)}(\bar
zX+Y)^n\,d\bar z$. The displayed cusp-form existence and zero equivalence are the formal assertion;
the differential identity explains its convention.

## Explanation of the source proof

Let $h\in S_k(\Gamma_1(N))$. There is a cusp form $v$ of the same weight and level satisfying

$$v(z)=\overline{h(-\bar z)},\qquad v=0\Longleftrightarrow h=0.$$

Use the full $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb R)$ slash action with

$$J=\begin{pmatrix}-1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad v=h|_kJ.$$

Mathlib's `CuspForm.translate` already proves that this slash action preserves holomorphy and
cuspidality, transporting the level to the conjugate subgroup. This includes negative determinants:
the slash action conjugates the value of the function while the action on the upper half-plane is
antiholomorphic.

The level is unchanged because

$$J\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix}J^{-1}
=\begin{pmatrix}a&-b\\-c&d\end{pmatrix}.$$

This integral matrix has determinant one, and preserves the conditions $a\equiv d\equiv1$ and
$c\equiv0\pmod N$. Conjugation is involutive, so it gives equality of the two level subgroups.
Transporting `CuspForm.translate h J` along that equality yields a cusp form at precisely the
original level.

For $J$, the absolute determinant and automorphy denominator are both one, and its action on the
upper half-plane is $z\mapsto-\bar z$. The slash formula therefore gives the required pointwise
identity. Since this reflection is an involution and complex conjugation is injective, $v=0$ implies
$h=0$; the converse follows immediately from the same formula.

This proof uses no open theorem imports. The hypotheses $N>0$ and $k\ge2$ are not needed for
reflection itself, but are retained to match the target statement.

